
==============================================================================
[1] WHAT CBAR IS: the Ursell tree-graph constant, not an animal/entropy constant
==============================================================================
definition (X01-L1C:71):  |phi^U(Gamma)| <= Cbar^{|Gamma|} u^{|Gamma|}
  phi^U(Gamma) = Ursell/Mayer polynomial of a connected cluster Gamma
  |Gamma|      = number of plaquettes in Gamma (one factor u = c_f/N each)
  => Cbar      = sup_Gamma ( |phi^U(Gamma)| / u^{|Gamma|} )^{1/|Gamma|}
  ubar := e*Delta*Cbar*u is then exactly the KP l.h.s.  sum_{g ni x}|w(g)|e^{a|g|}  (Z02-SEILER:68)

numerical reading: ubar=0.57 at (SU(2), beta_W=1/12) with e*Delta=54.365637, u=0.0104
  => Cbar_implied = ubar/(e*Delta*u) = 1.0000   (i.e. Cbar=1 is ASSUMED, never proved)

==============================================================================
[2] PROVABLE BOUND I: naive Cayley tree count does NOT give a constant (shows what is at stake)
==============================================================================
 n   n^{n-2}          (n^{n-2})^{1/n}   [per-vertex envelope; blow-up]
   1  1.0              1.0
   2  1.0              1.0
   3  3.0              1.4422496
   5  125.0            2.6265278
  10  100000           6.3095734
  20  2.6214           14.822689
  50  3.5527           42.757392
 100  1.0e+1           91.201084
 => envelope grows like n/e : NO size-independent Cbar from Cayley alone.
    (the tree entropy must be paid by the lattice position sum -> see [3];
     this is exactly why a *graph* bound, not a labelled-tree count, is the right route.)

==============================================================================
[3] PROVABLE BOUND II: Kirchhoff + AM-GM on the plaquette-adjacency graph  =>  Cbar <= Delta = 20
==============================================================================
 n     (Delta^{n-1}/n)^{1/n}     2*Delta-per-vertex
     1   1.0                       2.0
     2   3.16227766                6.32455532
     3   5.108729549               10.2174591
     5   7.962143411               15.92428682
    10   11.77408037               23.54816075
    50   17.4192718                34.8385436
   500   19.63495886               39.26991772
  5000   19.95400128               39.90800255
 => PROVABLE:  Cbar <= Delta = 20.0   (crude: Cbar <= 2*Delta = 40.0)

 non-vacuity check: tau of an m x m planar plaquette grid (n=m^2), and (tau)^{1/n}:
   m=2  n= 4  tau=4.0                 tau^{1/n}=1.4142136
   m=3  n= 9  tau=192.0               tau^{1/n}=1.7934949
   m=4  n=16  tau=100352.0            tau^{1/n}=2.0539761
   m=6  n=36  tau=3.25655396352e+13   tau^{1/n}=2.3733096
 (grids are far below the envelope 20 -- the bound is safe and loose by ~2-3x;
  no claim that 20 is attained, only that it is provable.)

==============================================================================
[4] N-SCALING: Cbar is O(1) in N (provable); the reading Cbar = N^2 is a DOUBLE COUNT
==============================================================================
 Cbar from [3] depends on Delta only:  dCbar/dN = 0  (Delta is a lattice degree, not an N-power)
 => Cbar = O(1);  ubar = e*Delta*Cbar*u ~ Cbar*beta/(2N^2)*O(1)  =>  beta_1 ~ N^2 (Wilson beta)
    the N^2 reading (Z02-SEILER sec.4 route D) reproduces the u=c_f normalisation:
    e*Delta*N^2*c_f/N = e*Delta*N*c_f  == route A  =>  identical criterion, only bookkeeping.
    Taking BOTH u=c_f/N and Cbar=N^2 pays the per-link 1/N twice. Rejected. [T]

 exact SU(2) c_f(beta) vs the two normalisations (window remark, AGENTS sec.2.1):
   beta=0.01       c_f=0.005000041667       b/(2N)=0.0025               b/N=0.005
   beta=0.020833333 c_f=0.01041704343        b/(2N)=0.005208333333       b/N=0.01041666667
   beta=0.1        c_f=0.05004167969        b/(2N)=0.025                b/N=0.05
   => at N=2 (f ~ fbar) c_f = beta/N + O(beta^3) = 2*(beta/2N): the repo's c_f=beta/(2N)
      is the N>=3 statement. This makes u 2x LARGER at N=2 => window 2x SMALLER (direction safe).

